EXPERIMENT 8
Deploy a Static Site via CI/CD Pipeline
Step 1: Open GitHub Repository
Open the browser.
Go to GitHub⁠�.
Log in to your GitHub account.
Open your repository: GitExperiment1.
Click the Code tab.
Step 2: Verify the Website Files
Check whether index.html exists in the repository.
Ensure the website contains the required HTML, CSS, and JavaScript files, if applicable.
Open index.html and verify its content.
Step 3: Open GitHub Pages Settings
Click Settings in the repository.
Select Pages from the left-side menu.
Check the Build and deployment section.
Verify that the website is being built from the main branch.
Note: In our experiment, GitHub Pages was already enabled, so we did not need to change the settings.
Step 4: Open the Workflow Directory
Return to the repository's Code tab.
Open the .github folder.
Open the workflows folder.
Check whether deploy.yml already exists.
Step 5: Verify the Deployment Workflow
Open the existing deploy.yml file.
Check its workflow configuration.
Do not create a duplicate file if deploy.yml already exists.
Do not overwrite an existing working workflow unnecessarily.
Step 6: Open GitHub Actions
Click the Actions tab.
Find the deployment workflow, such as pages build and deployment.
Open the latest workflow run.
Check whether the workflow completed successfully.
Step 7: Verify the Deployment Status
Check the latest workflow run.
Confirm that a green tick (✓) is displayed.
A green tick indicates that the deployment workflow completed successfully.
Step 8: Test Automatic Deployment
Return to the Code tab.
Open index.html.
Click the Edit (✏️) button.
Make a small change to the existing text.
Click Commit changes.
Enter a commit message, such as Update website content.
Commit the change directly to the main branch.
Open the Actions tab and check the latest deployment run.
Wait for deployment to finish successfully.
Step 9: Access the Deployed Website
Open the repository's Settings → Pages.
Click the published website link.
Refresh the website.
Verify that your changes appear.
